1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,515
Most people assume that running an artificial intelligence is pure, raw

2
00:00:04,565 --> 00:00:09,080
computation—billions of mathematical equations crunching numbers at the speed of light.

3
00:00:09,130 --> 00:00:16,550
If you inspect a forty-thousand-dollar state-of-the-art GPU during text generation, however, you will discover a shocking secret: the

4
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:23,605
most advanced silicon processors on earth spend more than ninety-nine percent of their time sitting completely idle.

5
00:00:23,655 --> 00:00:26,095
This is the infamous Memory Wall.

6
00:00:26,145 --> 00:00:30,660
When an AI reads your initial prompt, it processes all words

7
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:34,810
at once in a parallel burst of dense matrix multiplication.

8
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:37,300
Its computing engines are fully saturated.

9
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:40,205
But when it begins generating answers, it

10
00:00:40,255 --> 00:00:43,110
produces exactly one token at a time.

11
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:50,165
To generate a single word, the processor must transfer the model's entire memory footprint—hundreds of gigabytes of

12
00:00:50,215 --> 00:00:56,805
parameters—out of memory chips and into the compute cores, just to perform a single arithmetic step.

13
00:00:56,855 --> 00:00:59,295
The processor is not compute-bound; it

14
00:00:59,345 --> 00:01:01,370
is suffocating on memory bandwidth.

15
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:03,860
And without a crucial engineering breakthrough,

16
00:01:03,910 --> 00:01:06,350
real-time AI would be physically impossible.

17
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:09,341
To understand why text generation is so

18
00:01:09,391 --> 00:01:12,333
expensive, look inside the Transformer's self-attention mechanism.

19
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:16,608
Every single word must calculate its mathematical relationship with every

20
00:01:16,658 --> 00:01:20,454
previous word by projecting them into Keys and Values.

21
00:01:20,504 --> 00:01:22,164
Imagine writing a book.

22
00:01:22,214 --> 00:01:28,148
To write word number five hundred, you first stop and re-read all four hundred

23
00:01:28,198 --> 00:01:33,704
and ninety-nine previous words, calculating the grammar and context between every single pair.

24
00:01:33,754 --> 00:01:36,696
To write word five hundred and one,

25
00:01:36,746 --> 00:01:39,688
you repeat the entire process from scratch.

26
00:01:39,738 --> 00:01:43,108
Without optimization, generating a thousand tokens requires a

27
00:01:43,158 --> 00:01:46,100
quadratic explosion of calculations—O of N squared.

28
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:48,664
The solution is the Key-Value Cache.

29
00:01:48,714 --> 00:01:52,938
Once the model calculates the Key and Value vectors for

30
00:01:52,988 --> 00:01:56,785
past tokens, it saves them in high-speed GPU memory.

31
00:01:56,835 --> 00:02:01,059
When generating the next word, it only calculates a single

32
00:02:01,109 --> 00:02:04,906
Query vector and compares it against the stored cache.

33
00:02:04,956 --> 00:02:07,898
Generation becomes O of one per step.

34
00:02:07,948 --> 00:02:11,318
But saving all those past thoughts introduces a

35
00:02:11,368 --> 00:02:14,309
terrifying new monster: the exponential memory tax.

36
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,570
How much memory does a thought actually take?

37
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:20,423
Let’s look at the cold arithmetic of

38
00:02:20,473 --> 00:02:23,276
a standard seventy-billion-parameter open model like Llama.

39
00:02:23,326 --> 00:02:27,759
The network contains eighty transformer layers, sixty-four attention heads, a head

40
00:02:27,809 --> 00:02:31,835
dimension of one hundred and twenty-eight, stored in sixteen-bit precision.

41
00:02:31,885 --> 00:02:35,503
Multiply that out, and the Key-Value cache consumes two

42
00:02:35,553 --> 00:02:39,171
point six two megabytes for every single token generated.

43
00:02:39,221 --> 00:02:44,062
For a modest conversation of four thousand tokens, the KV cache alone

44
00:02:44,112 --> 00:02:48,953
demands more than ten gigabytes of dedicated memory for a single user.

45
00:02:49,003 --> 00:02:56,289
Expand that to a modern context window of one hundred and twenty-eight thousand tokens, and the cache swells

46
00:02:56,339 --> 00:03:03,218
to over three hundred and forty gigabytes—consuming more memory than the entire seventy-billion-parameter neural network weights combined!

47
00:03:03,268 --> 00:03:05,663
When multiple users query the system

48
00:03:05,713 --> 00:03:07,701
simultaneously, the memory footprints collide.

49
00:03:07,751 --> 00:03:11,369
The GPU runs out of VRAM, and the system

50
00:03:11,419 --> 00:03:14,629
crashes with the dreaded Out of Memory error.

51
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:18,112
The memory crisis was made far worse by

52
00:03:18,162 --> 00:03:21,159
how operating systems traditionally allocate GPU memory.

53
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:26,819
Because an AI cannot predict how many words a user will request, serving

54
00:03:26,869 --> 00:03:32,478
systems had to reserve contiguous blocks of memory for the worst-case maximum length.

55
00:03:32,528 --> 00:03:37,702
Imagine a hotel where every single guest might stay for eight thousand

56
00:03:37,752 --> 00:03:42,491
nights, so the hotel reserves an entire floor exclusively for them.

57
00:03:42,541 --> 00:03:46,409
Even if they check out after five minutes, no

58
00:03:46,459 --> 00:03:50,327
other guest is allowed to use those empty rooms.

59
00:03:50,377 --> 00:03:54,245
Early serving systems wasted up to eighty percent of

60
00:03:54,295 --> 00:03:57,727
total GPU memory on internal and external fragmentation!

61
00:03:57,778 --> 00:04:00,775
In 2023, researchers at UC Berkeley introduced

62
00:04:00,825 --> 00:04:03,387
PagedAttention, the core engine behind vLLM.

63
00:04:03,437 --> 00:04:09,481
Borrowing the legendary concept of virtual memory and paging from classical operating systems, PagedAttention

64
00:04:09,532 --> 00:04:15,141
slices the KV cache into small, fixed-size blocks of sixteen or thirty-two tokens.

65
00:04:15,191 --> 00:04:19,059
These blocks can be scattered anywhere in non-contiguous physical

66
00:04:19,109 --> 00:04:22,541
memory, mapped dynamically by a virtual block table.

67
00:04:22,591 --> 00:04:27,765
Memory waste plummeted to under four percent, quadrupling the number of simultaneous

68
00:04:27,816 --> 00:04:32,990
users a single GPU could serve without buying a single new chip.

69
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:36,325
Even with PagedAttention, transferring data between memory chips

70
00:04:36,375 --> 00:04:39,243
and compute cores remains a brutal bottleneck.

71
00:04:39,293 --> 00:04:47,165
On a modern graphics card, there are two distinct worlds: massive High Bandwidth Memory, which holds tens of gigabytes

72
00:04:47,215 --> 00:04:55,086
but is comparatively slow, and tiny on-chip SRAM cache, which is lightning fast but holds only a few megabytes.

73
00:04:55,136 --> 00:04:58,422
Standard attention calculations constantly write intermediate attention matrices

74
00:04:58,472 --> 00:05:01,757
back and forth across the slow memory bus.

75
00:05:01,807 --> 00:05:04,676
Enter FlashAttention, pioneered by researchers at Stanford.

76
00:05:04,726 --> 00:05:10,513
Instead of computing the entire attention matrix at once, FlashAttention tiles the input tokens

77
00:05:10,563 --> 00:05:15,933
into small bite-sized blocks that fit entirely inside the fast on-chip SRAM cache.

78
00:05:15,983 --> 00:05:21,770
Using a mathematical insight called Online Softmax, it updates running maximums and normalizes probabilities

79
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:27,606
on the fly, without ever writing the massive quadratic attention matrix into slow memory.

80
00:05:27,656 --> 00:05:33,026
By respecting the physical layout of the silicon, FlashAttention accelerates Transformer inference and

81
00:05:33,076 --> 00:05:38,030
training by up to four times, with zero loss in mathematical precision.

82
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,789
Today, the frontier of AI inference

83
00:05:40,839 --> 00:05:43,548
combines algorithmic intelligence with hardware physics.

84
00:05:43,598 --> 00:05:49,066
Architectures now use Grouped-Query Attention, where dozens of attention heads share the

85
00:05:49,116 --> 00:05:54,124
same Key and Value cache, slashing memory bandwidth requirements by eight-fold.

86
00:05:54,174 --> 00:05:58,723
And in Speculative Decoding, we deploy a small, nimble draft

87
00:05:58,773 --> 00:06:03,322
model to sprint ahead and quickly propose multiple candidate words.

88
00:06:03,372 --> 00:06:06,541
The giant, powerful model then verifies all

89
00:06:06,591 --> 00:06:09,760
candidates simultaneously in a single parallel computation.

90
00:06:09,810 --> 00:06:16,198
Because verifying multiple tokens in parallel takes nearly the same time as generating one,

91
00:06:16,248 --> 00:06:22,176
inference speeds double or triple without sacrificing a single drop of reasoning quality.

92
00:06:22,226 --> 00:06:25,395
Understanding artificial intelligence is not just about

93
00:06:25,445 --> 00:06:28,154
understanding neural weights and training algorithms.

94
00:06:28,204 --> 00:06:32,293
It is about understanding the physics of silicon, the

95
00:06:32,343 --> 00:06:36,431
flow of electrons, and the elegant architecture of memory.

96
00:06:36,481 --> 00:06:39,190
When algorithms honor the machine, intelligence

97
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:41,950
moves at the speed of thought.
